WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.780
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:14.674
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:00:14.674 --> 00:00:15.174
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:21.383
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for joining us for
the first view from the top of the year.

00:00:21.383 --> 00:00:26.244
Marcos, Herman, Solio, we are excited
that you can help kick it off for

00:00:26.244 --> 00:00:29.368
us during your 20th year reunion, no less.

00:00:29.368 --> 00:00:31.696
Actually, I believe the class
of 1999 is in the building.

00:00:31.696 --> 00:00:33.712
Can you guys make some noise so
we know you're here?

00:00:33.712 --> 00:00:37.723
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Okay, So

00:00:37.723 --> 00:00:44.437
I thought we could start by taking
a brisk walk down memory lane.

00:00:44.437 --> 00:00:48.632
I prepared a little flashback
Friday photo of you guys.

00:00:48.632 --> 00:00:50.461
Can we queue that photo, please?

00:00:50.461 --> 00:00:55.676
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:55.676 --> 00:00:58.279
&gt;&gt; This is an early Mercado Libre team

00:00:58.279 --> 00:01:00.170
shot, circa 1999.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:04.475
What I love about this one is as you guys
can see startup founder co fashion hasn't

00:01:04.475 --> 00:01:06.011
really evolved since then.

00:01:06.011 --> 00:01:08.421
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So A for consistency.

00:01:08.421 --> 00:01:12.424
Okay, we are going to jump right in
because we have three times as much wisdom

00:01:12.424 --> 00:01:13.438
to extract today.

00:01:13.438 --> 00:01:17.843
I want to start by talking through
your academic and career journeys.

00:01:17.843 --> 00:01:23.692
You each have very unique paths that
led you to Mercado Libre and beyond.

00:01:23.692 --> 00:01:25.051
Marcus, we'll start with you.

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:27.531
You have a background in finance and

00:01:27.531 --> 00:01:31.015
economics with a degree
from the Wharton School.

00:01:31.015 --> 00:01:34.509
But you also almost played
professional rugby.

00:01:34.509 --> 00:01:37.993
So tell us what was going on there,
you pass up the opportunity to play for

00:01:37.993 --> 00:01:38.976
the national team.

00:01:38.976 --> 00:01:42.197
Is it because you knew you were
destined for entrepreneurship?

00:01:42.197 --> 00:01:45.952
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] No, I think it's probably
because I knew I wasn't going to be that

00:01:45.952 --> 00:01:47.196
good of a rugby player.

00:01:47.196 --> 00:01:49.994
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Okay, Hernan,

00:01:49.994 --> 00:01:53.368
you did brand managements
at Procter &amp; Gamble,

00:01:53.368 --> 00:01:58.815
financial analysis at the UN, cofinder
MercadoLibre then started a VC firm.

00:01:58.815 --> 00:02:03.204
What did you want to be when you grew up
and what was the moment that you figured

00:02:03.204 --> 00:02:06.210
out, okay,
this is what I am supposed to be doing?

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:09.698
&gt;&gt; I think,
I always wanted to be an entrepreneur.

00:02:09.698 --> 00:02:15.231
There was something that I Imagined
it happening later in my life.

00:02:15.231 --> 00:02:17.798
And I think that being here at Stanford,

00:02:17.798 --> 00:02:22.329
that particular moment in time also
having met Marcos and everything,

00:02:22.329 --> 00:02:27.860
accelerated that really 20 years, and
ended up happening right after graduation.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:28.754
&gt;&gt; Can you tell us the story?

00:02:28.754 --> 00:02:31.525
How did you all meet?

00:02:31.525 --> 00:02:35.512
&gt;&gt; So-
&gt;&gt; And I met before Stanford actually.

00:02:35.512 --> 00:02:37.615
And we became friends here at school.

00:02:37.615 --> 00:02:42.320
We're a group of Latin Americans always
discussing about the things that we

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:47.115
should be doing about the Internet,
this Internet thing that was starting.

00:02:47.115 --> 00:02:50.220
And obviously no one was ever doing
anything at some point in time.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.777
We said, why don't we just go and do it?

00:02:52.777 --> 00:02:56.390
&gt;&gt; We're also arguing about soccer and
who was the best.

00:02:56.390 --> 00:02:59.178
We got that going on as well,
I should say.

00:02:59.178 --> 00:03:00.031
As a token Brazilian here.

00:03:00.031 --> 00:03:04.266
&gt;&gt; We no longer discuss that,
unfortunately.

00:03:04.266 --> 00:03:07.571
&gt;&gt; Stileo, you have the highest number
of Stanford degree in this stage.

00:03:07.571 --> 00:03:11.760
Can you tell us about the inspiration
from moving from the technical

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.895
world to the world of business
management and entrepreneurship?

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:21.723
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I was really thinking about
finance before coming to Stanford,

00:03:21.723 --> 00:03:24.832
joining Stanford and after Stanford.

00:03:24.832 --> 00:03:28.808
The way I got to MercadoLibre
was through casual

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:33.393
meeting with Marcos at Lehman
brothers of all places.

00:03:33.393 --> 00:03:37.683
Three months after graduating
from Stanford, he was visiting,

00:03:37.683 --> 00:03:39.640
he was fundraising already.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:40.226
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:03:40.226 --> 00:03:44.544
&gt;&gt; And we met and
I was convinced by his passion,

00:03:44.544 --> 00:03:48.982
the passion that he had
to build this company.

00:03:48.982 --> 00:03:54.737
I called him, and three weeks later,
I and my wife who's here,

00:03:54.737 --> 00:04:01.258
moved from New York to Sao Paulo to
start the business there in Sao Paulo.

00:04:01.258 --> 00:04:05.896
&gt;&gt; So today, we're going to dig deep into
themes of entrepreneurship and adversity.

00:04:05.896 --> 00:04:08.634
But first, I want to start by taking
us back to where it all began.

00:04:08.634 --> 00:04:10.963
So it's the spring of 1999.

00:04:10.963 --> 00:04:13.415
Ricky Martin is
an international sensation.

00:04:13.415 --> 00:04:15.054
The Euro was just invented.

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:16.898
It's overall a really good year.

00:04:16.898 --> 00:04:21.552
Especially, because the idea for
MercadoLibre was just born.

00:04:21.552 --> 00:04:23.147
Now it's a huge success.

00:04:23.147 --> 00:04:26.689
It was worth almost 28 billion as of
the last time I checked this morning.

00:04:26.689 --> 00:04:32.976
But can you take us back to day zero and
how did you get from day zero to day one?

00:04:32.976 --> 00:04:36.640
What were some of the baby steps that
you took to execute while you were still

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:38.920
students on campus
leading up to graduation?

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:42.966
&gt;&gt; Well, first I spent a lot of time
in the library and that's where Stilio

00:04:42.966 --> 00:04:47.435
became suspicious, because he wasn't
used to see me at the library, right?

00:04:47.435 --> 00:04:49.708
The second time he saw me at the library,
he said,

00:04:49.708 --> 00:04:51.454
I think you're planning something.

00:04:51.454 --> 00:04:52.042
&gt;&gt; Onto something.

00:04:52.042 --> 00:04:57.214
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's a true story, by the way.

00:04:57.214 --> 00:04:59.903
So, yeah, I mean, obviously,

00:04:59.903 --> 00:05:05.195
took the opportunity to drive
John Muse to the airport, as Dean.

00:05:05.195 --> 00:05:07.372
Living was just saying and

00:05:07.372 --> 00:05:12.624
was able to get some soft commitments
to start in the business.

00:05:12.624 --> 00:05:15.031
Then I convinced Hernan.

00:05:15.031 --> 00:05:17.865
Hernan was going to be the CEO of
another startup and I told him,

00:05:17.865 --> 00:05:20.070
why don't you just do
the marketing plan for me?

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:21.651
I knew he would have.

00:05:21.651 --> 00:05:24.590
Once I got him in,
he would never leave, so

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:27.468
I had to convince him some way or another.

00:05:27.468 --> 00:05:32.148
And, yeah, so I tried to assemble
the team and do as much research and

00:05:32.148 --> 00:05:36.047
tried to get as much commitments
to funding as possible.

00:05:36.047 --> 00:05:40.829
&gt;&gt; Okay, and it couldn't have been
easy starting a company as students.

00:05:40.829 --> 00:05:45.037
I want to dive a little bit deeper into
some of the team dynamics of starting

00:05:45.037 --> 00:05:46.975
a company with your classmates.

00:05:46.975 --> 00:05:50.926
I know many of us are in this situation
right now, or at least considering it.

00:05:50.926 --> 00:05:53.531
And we're having very,
very tough conversations.

00:05:53.531 --> 00:05:57.627
So can you please tell us, how did
you decide who was going to be CEO?

00:05:57.627 --> 00:06:00.318
What was that equity
split conversation like?

00:06:00.318 --> 00:06:01.570
What were those dynamics?

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:07.949
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I warned them ahead of
time about tough questions, so.

00:06:07.949 --> 00:06:10.458
&gt;&gt; In our case, it was the first part,

00:06:10.458 --> 00:06:15.656
which I think is the most important
part is you need to get along very well.

00:06:15.656 --> 00:06:21.836
Hernan, myself, Marquino Santos was here,
was there and was also working with us.

00:06:21.836 --> 00:06:26.552
We always hanged out together and
we spent a lot of time together.

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:31.052
So I knew that it was going to work well.

00:06:31.052 --> 00:06:35.895
So I think you need to start a company,
obviously with somebody who compliments

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:39.016
you, but
also someone who you get along well with.

00:06:39.016 --> 00:06:40.752
And then the equity split, etc.

00:06:40.752 --> 00:06:43.462
They're all different kind of solutions.

00:06:43.462 --> 00:06:47.339
In our case, I started it and then I was
going to be CEO at different company.

00:06:47.339 --> 00:06:53.250
I convinced him to do the marketing plans
and then we agreed on an equity package.

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:56.680
Let's say you shouldn't do what
our competitors from Harvard did.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:00.635
They were 10 guys and
they split 10% each, so

00:07:00.635 --> 00:07:06.679
each one had 10% of the company and
it didn't work out very well for them.

00:07:06.679 --> 00:07:09.394
&gt;&gt; Tell us more about
those Harvard competitors.

00:07:09.394 --> 00:07:12.108
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:12.108 --> 00:07:13.960
&gt;&gt; I know you love this story, Hernan.

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:18.709
&gt;&gt; It was interesting because
if there's one thing that it was

00:07:18.709 --> 00:07:23.556
very innovative,
the idea because at the time we launched.

00:07:23.556 --> 00:07:29.075
There were like 80 other companies
throughout Latin America more or

00:07:29.075 --> 00:07:32.358
less following the same business model.

00:07:32.358 --> 00:07:36.734
The difference in the case of
MercadoLibre was long term visual and

00:07:36.734 --> 00:07:41.674
assembling a great team, the kind of
commitment that that team had, etc.

00:07:41.674 --> 00:07:43.525
But not the idea per say.

00:07:43.525 --> 00:07:44.813
And in particular,

00:07:44.813 --> 00:07:49.600
there was this other Dean from Harvard
that started right at the same time.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:55.484
It's a funny story because we organized
our launching ceremony in Buenos Aires and

00:07:55.484 --> 00:07:57.334
just out of coincidence,

00:07:57.334 --> 00:08:02.305
those guys had Reserved the same place
the following day or the day before.

00:08:02.305 --> 00:08:07.361
I don't remember how it was, and they
were sure that we did that on purpose,

00:08:07.361 --> 00:08:11.235
but it was just a coincidence,
and we started competing.

00:08:11.235 --> 00:08:16.555
They ended up raising initially
more capital than we did,

00:08:16.555 --> 00:08:19.171
so they had some advantage.

00:08:19.171 --> 00:08:24.393
But their focus was more on marketing,
on trying really

00:08:24.393 --> 00:08:29.627
to hit some initial milestones and
go public very soon.

00:08:29.627 --> 00:08:34.116
While ours was more around
trying to build a technology,

00:08:34.116 --> 00:08:40.209
trying to bring build organic engine for
growth and ended up paying out well.

00:08:40.209 --> 00:08:46.131
In particular, when at the time we
started my career was when everything

00:08:46.131 --> 00:08:52.557
was really colorful, then everything
went into gray after the bubble burst.

00:08:52.557 --> 00:08:57.289
And that when you started to see their
big big difference between the two teams.

00:08:57.289 --> 00:09:01.336
And a few years later we
ended up buying that company

00:09:01.336 --> 00:09:05.961
a refraction of the capital
they had raised in the market.

00:09:05.961 --> 00:09:08.435
&gt;&gt; So, Stanford, Harvard.

00:09:08.435 --> 00:09:09.028
&gt;&gt; There you go.

00:09:09.028 --> 00:09:12.684
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I want to get your thoughts

00:09:12.684 --> 00:09:14.795
on the team dynamics because, as MBAs,

00:09:14.795 --> 00:09:18.393
we often hear that we're too type A and
you shouldn't start a company

00:09:18.393 --> 00:09:21.769
with multiple MBA co-founders
because it will be too intense.

00:09:21.769 --> 00:09:25.495
What was it like for you joining the team
and building a company with your friends?

00:09:25.495 --> 00:09:27.337
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I think it was a great experience.

00:09:27.337 --> 00:09:33.519
And for me, the fact that I was in
Brazil and I had a clear mandate,

00:09:33.519 --> 00:09:39.044
which was to build the business
there gave me some leeway.

00:09:39.044 --> 00:09:42.228
Obviously, we interacted a lot.

00:09:42.228 --> 00:09:48.830
So there's balance there between having
the chance to really hire my team,

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:53.691
build my team, but
also work together with my friends.

00:09:53.691 --> 00:09:57.688
&gt;&gt; So I want to stay on our GSB
experience a little bit because it's so

00:09:57.688 --> 00:09:59.445
relevant to many of us here.

00:09:59.445 --> 00:10:02.619
Stileo, what specifically in
those two years really shaped you

00:10:02.619 --> 00:10:03.746
as a business leader?

00:10:03.746 --> 00:10:04.953
What did you experience?

00:10:04.953 --> 00:10:05.981
What did you learn?

00:10:05.981 --> 00:10:12.602
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I, typically, when I speak
to students say don't follow my example.

00:10:12.602 --> 00:10:13.221
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:10:13.221 --> 00:10:14.395
&gt;&gt; Which is good.

00:10:14.395 --> 00:10:18.668
I mean at least, it's something that
people should learn which is I was

00:10:18.668 --> 00:10:20.531
very much focused on finance.

00:10:20.531 --> 00:10:25.309
I came to Stanford already with
a background in finance but

00:10:25.309 --> 00:10:28.439
not with the knowledge with a theory.

00:10:28.439 --> 00:10:34.214
And so I took all finance classes,
some really PhD level classes and

00:10:34.214 --> 00:10:41.226
the like, and ended up working my summer
in finance and then after school as well.

00:10:41.226 --> 00:10:42.626
And don't do that.

00:10:42.626 --> 00:10:45.010
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Really, please, don't.

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:50.152
This is the chance for
you to experiment for you to go outside.

00:10:50.152 --> 00:10:53.629
What is your comfort zone
to try different classes,

00:10:53.629 --> 00:10:56.483
really learn skills that you don't have.

00:10:56.483 --> 00:11:01.291
But I think the most important thing is
building relationships, and that I did,

00:11:01.291 --> 00:11:04.909
and that's why we're here,
I guess, telling this story.

00:11:04.909 --> 00:11:05.827
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:11:05.827 --> 00:11:10.223
Stileo just mentioned building
relationships, and I know Dean told

00:11:10.223 --> 00:11:14.866
the story of how professor Jack McDonald
really helps in the beginning.

00:11:14.866 --> 00:11:18.645
When you think about the people who
had your back while you were here,

00:11:18.645 --> 00:11:21.445
who were those champions and
how did you develop and

00:11:21.445 --> 00:11:23.878
cultivate those relationships over time?

00:11:23.878 --> 00:11:28.363
&gt;&gt; Well, obviously for me, that class
from Jack McDonald was very important.

00:11:28.363 --> 00:11:32.749
I had used my silver bullet for
his class, and in those days,

00:11:32.749 --> 00:11:35.254
you had to use your silver bullet.

00:11:35.254 --> 00:11:38.739
I spend most of my time
here going to class.

00:11:38.739 --> 00:11:42.493
I really enjoyed going to class but
I didn't spend that much time studying for

00:11:42.493 --> 00:11:43.897
exams and things like that.

00:11:43.897 --> 00:11:44.585
&gt;&gt; At the library.

00:11:44.585 --> 00:11:46.574
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Or at the library.

00:11:46.574 --> 00:11:51.783
So I really enjoyed because Jack McDonald
would bring amazing guests to his lessons.

00:11:51.783 --> 00:11:53.682
That's why I use his silver bullet.

00:11:53.682 --> 00:11:58.581
I would always sit in the front row.

00:11:58.581 --> 00:12:03.254
Once I got into the class and
it was early, I always got in early to get

00:12:03.254 --> 00:12:07.779
into the front row, and
there was a guy just sitting at the table.

00:12:07.779 --> 00:12:11.597
And it was just him and me for
like two, three minutes,

00:12:11.597 --> 00:12:16.418
and he said I'm Joe Doe, I just
dressed up like Warren Buffet today.

00:12:16.418 --> 00:12:21.325
And I had no idea who he was, so
I didn't understand the joke either, and

00:12:21.325 --> 00:12:23.193
the guy was Warren Buffet.

00:12:23.193 --> 00:12:27.192
And I was looking for
funding for my company, so

00:12:27.192 --> 00:12:31.796
then eventually,
our classmates started to walk in.

00:12:31.796 --> 00:12:36.134
I realized everybody recognized the guy,
the class started, it was a great class.

00:12:36.134 --> 00:12:40.504
And then after the class, I told Jack
McDonnell, Jack, I want to do a startup.

00:12:40.504 --> 00:12:43.397
I'm looking for
someone to fund this company.

00:12:43.397 --> 00:12:47.168
And I spent three minutes with
Warren Buffett alone in the class and

00:12:47.168 --> 00:12:48.998
wasn't even able to pitch him.

00:12:48.998 --> 00:12:50.395
Next time.

00:12:50.395 --> 00:12:54.501
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Next time someone like this comes

00:12:54.501 --> 00:12:56.145
through, let me know.

00:12:56.145 --> 00:13:00.853
I will [LAUGH] I will
attend your classes anyhow.

00:13:00.853 --> 00:13:02.511
I am always there in the front row.

00:13:02.511 --> 00:13:06.144
And he said, don't worry,
he would have invested anyhow.

00:13:06.144 --> 00:13:08.666
He doesn't invest in Latin America but
these guys,

00:13:08.666 --> 00:13:11.031
they have lots of investments
in Latin America.

00:13:11.031 --> 00:13:13.496
I would tell this guy about
you during lunch, and

00:13:13.496 --> 00:13:16.395
I would set you up to drive him
to the airport after class.

00:13:16.395 --> 00:13:21.429
So that relationship with Jack McDonald
really worked out very well for me.

00:13:21.429 --> 00:13:22.548
&gt;&gt; That's amazing.

00:13:22.548 --> 00:13:28.809
Hernan, what were your most salient
GSB memories of support and rejection?

00:13:28.809 --> 00:13:33.234
Those are two incredibly common and
useful experiences for self-growth but

00:13:33.234 --> 00:13:37.331
I don't think the latter is often
discussed, so please share with us.

00:13:37.331 --> 00:13:42.085
&gt;&gt; Being generally to an amazing
amazing experience for

00:13:42.085 --> 00:13:45.896
me, was very eye-opening in all senses.

00:13:45.896 --> 00:13:51.147
In terms of meeting these many people with

00:13:51.147 --> 00:13:56.408
such great ambitions, really amazing.

00:13:56.408 --> 00:14:01.492
And also for us, it was, again,
at a very particular moment in time

00:14:01.492 --> 00:14:06.415
where we basically presented us
to the technology opportunity.

00:14:06.415 --> 00:14:08.455
That was happening here and

00:14:08.455 --> 00:14:13.430
it made us realized that nobody
knew anything about the Internet.

00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:17.190
So we're all starting in
a level playing field,

00:14:17.190 --> 00:14:22.144
and that gave young professionals
with really lots of energy and

00:14:22.144 --> 00:14:26.655
willingness to change the world,
to start doing things.

00:14:26.655 --> 00:14:30.007
&gt;&gt; In any other industry would
have been much more difficult and

00:14:30.007 --> 00:14:34.673
I think that's why when they said I want
to be an entrepreneur in about 10 years 20

00:14:34.673 --> 00:14:38.683
years from now is because I thought
you needed first to build something,

00:14:38.683 --> 00:14:40.152
that is not what I wanted.

00:14:40.152 --> 00:14:47.593
But what I always regret from my days
here is that it was only a 24-hour day.

00:14:47.593 --> 00:14:52.120
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; There were many things to do.

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:56.265
All the opportunities to
be present with speakers,

00:14:56.265 --> 00:14:59.281
in classes with amazing professors,

00:14:59.281 --> 00:15:04.853
discussing things with classmates,
and you could not do all of them.

00:15:04.853 --> 00:15:09.436
It's a challenge, so
they all opportunities are amazing.

00:15:09.436 --> 00:15:15.087
It's about what you don't do and how you
make the best decisions best on that.

00:15:15.087 --> 00:15:16.219
And it's difficult.

00:15:16.219 --> 00:15:19.837
I think it's something
that them as managers,

00:15:19.837 --> 00:15:24.005
as business leaders,
we keep on facing what not to do.

00:15:24.005 --> 00:15:27.405
I think that is what a regr, in general,

00:15:27.405 --> 00:15:32.304
has been an amazing experience and
incredible journey and

00:15:32.304 --> 00:15:37.304
to be here sharing this with
some of our other classmates and

00:15:37.304 --> 00:15:40.607
current students is also incredible.

00:15:40.607 --> 00:15:43.492
I was thinking earlier kind of a joke but

00:15:43.492 --> 00:15:48.051
I remembered I was in this very
similar event with Steve Jobs.

00:15:48.051 --> 00:15:51.326
I know it's just the three to five here so
[LAUGH].

00:15:51.326 --> 00:15:55.560
You go devalued is
the Argentinian Peso [LAUGH].

00:15:55.560 --> 00:16:00.494
[LAUGH] Well, when you talk about
that time what you were here at GSP,

00:16:00.494 --> 00:16:04.217
it seems like it was such
the momentous occasion and

00:16:04.217 --> 00:16:07.792
the time to really jump
into entrepreneurship.

00:16:07.792 --> 00:16:09.478
At least, that's what it seems.

00:16:09.478 --> 00:16:12.690
But, Marcos,
you have this great quote that says,

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:16.975
they got tired of telling me that
my idea was not going to work.

00:16:16.975 --> 00:16:17.578
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:16:17.578 --> 00:16:21.769
&gt;&gt; How did you find the tenacity to take
that leap of faith despite overwhelming

00:16:21.769 --> 00:16:26.042
voices at the time telling you not to
move back to Latin America after the GSB?

00:16:26.042 --> 00:16:28.950
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And what advice do you have for us?

00:16:28.950 --> 00:16:33.192
I'm looking at the NBA teams right now
who are going to make similar decisions,

00:16:33.192 --> 00:16:35.882
many of which require us
to go against the green.

00:16:35.882 --> 00:16:40.099
&gt;&gt; Well, everybody, everyone here at the
GSB, all of the Latin students are new and

00:16:40.099 --> 00:16:43.241
everybody said this is never
going to work in Latin America.

00:16:43.241 --> 00:16:48.076
Never in Latin America someone is going to
buy a product they haven't seen or

00:16:48.076 --> 00:16:50.575
touched to someone they don't know.

00:16:50.575 --> 00:16:54.777
Because this was happening in Germany,
in Japan and in the US.

00:16:54.777 --> 00:16:57.556
This type of business model and
said this is for

00:16:57.556 --> 00:17:01.210
this type of cultures, so
these won't work in our culture.

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:03.143
And I was convinced it was going to work.

00:17:03.143 --> 00:17:06.864
Partly because having been
a Latin American who studied in the US,

00:17:06.864 --> 00:17:10.849
I realize how Latin Americans behave
when they're in Latin America but

00:17:10.849 --> 00:17:14.653
then how they behave when they're
here in a system that works well.

00:17:14.653 --> 00:17:19.217
So I just knew that we needed to create
a system that works well with clear rules

00:17:19.217 --> 00:17:20.353
and regulations.

00:17:20.353 --> 00:17:25.280
And I was convinced,
that didn't discourage me at all.

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:28.136
Actually, I was a little bit naive
because I thought, this is great.

00:17:28.136 --> 00:17:30.095
Everybody thinks it's not going to work.

00:17:30.095 --> 00:17:33.864
There won't be that much competition,
which as [INAUDIBLE] said before,

00:17:33.864 --> 00:17:34.785
it was a mistake.

00:17:34.785 --> 00:17:36.867
There was a lot of competition.

00:17:36.867 --> 00:17:40.347
But I would say as an advice,

00:17:40.347 --> 00:17:44.849
I would certainly try to take risks.

00:17:44.849 --> 00:17:49.419
If you have a view of something
that is a contrarian view but

00:17:49.419 --> 00:17:52.514
it makes sense to you, just pursue it.

00:17:52.514 --> 00:17:56.169
But then you need to be very resilient and
long term focused.

00:17:56.169 --> 00:18:00.511
I mean there's a lot of
bumps down the road and

00:18:00.511 --> 00:18:06.812
you need to be very resilient
because the bumps can be very tough.

00:18:06.812 --> 00:18:11.624
You need to have a great team and that's
what the relationships of the GSB for me.

00:18:11.624 --> 00:18:12.847
And for us, we're amazing.

00:18:12.847 --> 00:18:19.266
We still have many GSBers working for
us for over 20 years.

00:18:19.266 --> 00:18:24.559
So, you need a great team because
you cannot do it alone and

00:18:24.559 --> 00:18:28.571
it's also more fun if
you have a great team.

00:18:28.571 --> 00:18:33.972
But try to be contrarian with a view
that you really believe in and

00:18:33.972 --> 00:18:36.783
just take risks and hold on to it.

00:18:36.783 --> 00:18:41.542
&gt;&gt; And you could say that the contrarians
held that view because they were thinking

00:18:41.542 --> 00:18:46.089
about the economic landscape of the market
at the time on what they perceived

00:18:46.089 --> 00:18:47.241
were challenges.

00:18:47.241 --> 00:18:50.182
Today, when we look at Silicon Valley and
other parts of the world,

00:18:50.182 --> 00:18:52.724
it's saturated with every type
of start up under the sun.

00:18:52.724 --> 00:18:55.959
But when you guys started in
Latin America, that was not the case.

00:18:55.959 --> 00:18:59.575
There wasn't really a big culture
of tech entrepreneurship.

00:18:59.575 --> 00:19:03.040
VC dollars were not flowing
the way they are flowing today.

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:06.576
So how did you convince investors
to give you money back then?

00:19:06.576 --> 00:19:11.319
How did you make decisions
without not too much data?

00:19:11.319 --> 00:19:11.829
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it was hard.

00:19:11.829 --> 00:19:16.912
I mean, we started with VCs, and
actually investment banks invested in us.

00:19:16.912 --> 00:19:20.962
Which was a big problem later on
because after the bubble burst,

00:19:20.962 --> 00:19:25.162
some of them got cold feet and
they wanted to shut down the company.

00:19:25.162 --> 00:19:29.663
So we had to fight with our [COUGH]
shareholders not to shut down the company.

00:19:29.663 --> 00:19:34.648
We had to go to a shareholder vote, and
we were pretty close to losing that vote

00:19:34.648 --> 00:19:39.483
because given that we had raised a lot
of money and unlike our competitors,

00:19:39.483 --> 00:19:41.419
we hadn't spent that money.

00:19:41.419 --> 00:19:45.362
Some of our shareholders said,
let's shut down this company and

00:19:45.362 --> 00:19:50.024
take the money out, and it's going to be
the only investment in which at least we

00:19:50.024 --> 00:19:51.833
will not have lost the money.

00:19:51.833 --> 00:19:55.766
[LAUGH] So, yeah, when you get
investors that are not used to risk,

00:19:55.766 --> 00:19:59.572
it's not the best thing, but
it's the only thing we could get.

00:19:59.572 --> 00:20:03.723
Fortunately, now we have funds like
Hernan's that are funding lots of

00:20:03.723 --> 00:20:04.770
entrepreneurs.

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:08.658
And really, entrepreneurship has
taken off, and there are VCs,

00:20:08.658 --> 00:20:12.002
there are many organizations
that help entrepreneurs.

00:20:12.002 --> 00:20:17.077
So it's a very different landscape
today than what it was 20 years ago.

00:20:17.077 --> 00:20:18.684
&gt;&gt; Well if we fast forward,

00:20:18.684 --> 00:20:22.669
you just celebrated 20 years
of Mercado Libre's success.

00:20:22.669 --> 00:20:26.934
And during that time, you navigated and
thrived through multiple crises,

00:20:26.934 --> 00:20:29.475
both here in the States and
in Latin America.

00:20:29.475 --> 00:20:32.143
Even IPOing during one of those crises.

00:20:32.143 --> 00:20:35.541
And you were the first Latin American
company to be listed on the NASDAQ.

00:20:35.541 --> 00:20:37.115
That's incredible.

00:20:37.115 --> 00:20:41.146
Stileo, what have been the values
that you have held dear in the past

00:20:41.146 --> 00:20:45.474
two decades that have enabled you to
brace the ups and downs of this time?

00:20:45.474 --> 00:20:51.004
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I mentioned when I met
Marcos that one time at Lehman Brothers

00:20:51.004 --> 00:20:56.838
when I was convinced that I wanted to
join, there was passion in his eyes.

00:20:56.838 --> 00:21:01.638
When I spoke to Hernan later,
I also saw that same passion,

00:21:01.638 --> 00:21:05.779
and I think there is a sense
of purpose in what we do.

00:21:05.779 --> 00:21:09.538
We saw that technology was
changing people's lives and

00:21:09.538 --> 00:21:12.262
it was going to change people's lives.

00:21:12.262 --> 00:21:14.179
I think what Marcos was saying,

00:21:14.179 --> 00:21:18.162
this sort of contrarian view was
that there was going to be impact.

00:21:18.162 --> 00:21:21.781
Maybe the people that said
no were the people that

00:21:21.781 --> 00:21:25.676
were just seeing things
the way they were back then.

00:21:25.676 --> 00:21:30.164
And we were seeing things
sort of beyond that.

00:21:30.164 --> 00:21:34.925
And I think that sense of purpose,
that technology can change people's

00:21:34.925 --> 00:21:39.156
lives when we think of what we
do as an e-commerce marketplace.

00:21:39.156 --> 00:21:43.607
The number of entrepreneurs actually
that have built businesses on top

00:21:43.607 --> 00:21:44.729
of our platform.

00:21:44.729 --> 00:21:49.560
We're now also going into fintech and
I think there's tremendous

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:55.268
opportunity with financial services,
democratizing financial services.

00:21:55.268 --> 00:21:59.847
So those I think are values
that have kept us together and

00:21:59.847 --> 00:22:02.972
having that long term vision as well.

00:22:02.972 --> 00:22:07.601
&gt;&gt; Hernan, you were at Mercado Libre and
then you left to Found Cosmic ventures.

00:22:07.601 --> 00:22:12.535
When you look back at the last 20
years from your GSB time to now,

00:22:12.535 --> 00:22:17.228
what has been the single most
difficult part of that journey?

00:22:17.228 --> 00:22:23.230
&gt;&gt; Difficult part I think, going back to
a couple of instances in our career days.

00:22:23.230 --> 00:22:28.407
One was when we tried to close
our second round of financing,

00:22:28.407 --> 00:22:30.751
which we ended up closing.

00:22:30.751 --> 00:22:36.931
But we closed that one in May 2000,
and the bubble burst in March 2000.

00:22:36.931 --> 00:22:40.749
So I remember at the time we had
like probably 10 term sheets or

00:22:40.749 --> 00:22:44.654
10 investors really interested
in investing in the company.

00:22:44.654 --> 00:22:48.452
And as days went by,
one investor will drop out and

00:22:48.452 --> 00:22:54.439
another one another one another one, and
we ended up with one final investor.

00:22:54.439 --> 00:22:59.310
I remember organized a call with
us to discuss the marketing

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:04.189
plan of which was a very
tiny operation in America.

00:23:04.189 --> 00:23:07.559
We have the time management, well,
why do they want to do this?

00:23:07.559 --> 00:23:12.088
And we went into the meeting,
kind of hesitating what to say and

00:23:12.088 --> 00:23:16.544
basically, those investors,
they were also dropping out.

00:23:16.544 --> 00:23:19.081
So we're left with nothing.

00:23:19.081 --> 00:23:25.107
Somehow, I don't know, each one of
those say, games I do play 20 times.

00:23:25.107 --> 00:23:27.429
And probably,
you end up losing most of the them.

00:23:27.429 --> 00:23:31.691
But we would turn it around,
I think again, in persistence and

00:23:31.691 --> 00:23:36.771
also some help from the investors that
were inside the company at the time.

00:23:36.771 --> 00:23:42.885
We ended up closing that round, but
that was a very tough time for us.

00:23:42.885 --> 00:23:47.081
I remember we used to get in a room
together and say what the heck,

00:23:47.081 --> 00:23:48.107
what can we do?

00:23:48.107 --> 00:23:51.581
And then we would walk out the room and
say, hey, guys, everything's fine.

00:23:51.581 --> 00:23:56.313
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's terrible because also our

00:23:56.313 --> 00:24:00.732
competitors were doing a lot of marketing
and they were Everybody, even our friends,

00:24:00.732 --> 00:24:02.517
our competitors were called the remata.

00:24:02.517 --> 00:24:04.939
And even my friends would say,
so how's the remata doing?

00:24:04.939 --> 00:24:07.122
And we were like, no,
ours is MercadoLibre.

00:24:07.122 --> 00:24:10.811
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So at night, we would lock up ourselves

00:24:10.811 --> 00:24:16.720
up in a room and we were like, okay,
let's release our anguish now between us.

00:24:16.720 --> 00:24:19.951
And then we would leave the room and
everything is good, but it was.

00:24:19.951 --> 00:24:21.418
&gt;&gt; We'll close around [LAUGH].

00:24:21.418 --> 00:24:23.326
&gt;&gt; Yes, it was very, very tough.

00:24:23.326 --> 00:24:27.904
&gt;&gt; That was a tough time of what
Marcos described of that voting.

00:24:27.904 --> 00:24:34.510
I think was the only time that the board
ever voted for something in MercadoLibre.

00:24:34.510 --> 00:24:38.588
We're kind of sleeping with the enemy, who
were happy that the company was evolving

00:24:38.588 --> 00:24:40.953
and our own shareholders
wanted to shut it down.

00:24:40.953 --> 00:24:46.605
&gt;&gt; I want to dig deeper into the nuances
of building a business in Latin America.

00:24:46.605 --> 00:24:51.011
We know now that there's so much
opportunity, MercadoLibre has proved that,

00:24:51.011 --> 00:24:52.929
Kaszek Ventures has proven that.

00:24:52.929 --> 00:24:58.009
But we also keep hearing about all of
these challenges, perceived challenges.

00:24:58.009 --> 00:25:00.890
Which of these challenges
would you say are missed that

00:25:00.890 --> 00:25:02.592
you are tired of hearing about?

00:25:02.592 --> 00:25:04.261
And which of those would you say,

00:25:04.261 --> 00:25:07.313
truly these are aspects in which
the region is still behind?

00:25:09.726 --> 00:25:12.207
&gt;&gt; I would say all
the stereotypes are true.

00:25:12.207 --> 00:25:12.768
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:25:12.768 --> 00:25:17.392
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I was trying to use a narrative but-

00:25:17.392 --> 00:25:19.435
&gt;&gt; All the challenges are there, no,

00:25:19.435 --> 00:25:20.190
it's true.

00:25:20.190 --> 00:25:21.165
&gt;&gt; Give us some examples.

00:25:21.165 --> 00:25:22.447
&gt;&gt; It's tough, it's very tough.

00:25:22.447 --> 00:25:28.036
Infrastructure is not as developed,

00:25:28.036 --> 00:25:32.408
regulations are backwards.

00:25:32.408 --> 00:25:38.457
Many times, regulators are not thinking
for the benefit of most of the people.

00:25:38.457 --> 00:25:41.464
They're just trying to optimize for
themselves.

00:25:41.464 --> 00:25:46.513
So it's very few times,
you encounter an environment

00:25:46.513 --> 00:25:51.455
which is very pro-business and
pro-growth, etc,

00:25:51.455 --> 00:25:56.968
which it's part of the game,
so you need to deal with that.

00:25:56.968 --> 00:26:00.234
And at the end of the day,
if you are able to deal with that,

00:26:00.234 --> 00:26:02.388
the opportunity is still just as big.

00:26:02.388 --> 00:26:06.363
But you need to understand that
the environment is not necessarily

00:26:06.363 --> 00:26:10.701
a pro-business environment, and
it changes and it's very volatile.

00:26:10.701 --> 00:26:13.744
So, all of a sudden then you have
elections and a new government comes in,

00:26:13.744 --> 00:26:15.671
and it's great, and
it lasts for a few years.

00:26:15.671 --> 00:26:19.023
And then a different government
comes in and they change everything.

00:26:19.023 --> 00:26:23.902
I would say except for Chile, which is
a country that has been very stable and

00:26:23.902 --> 00:26:26.583
growing very, very well for many years.

00:26:26.583 --> 00:26:32.182
The rest of Latin America is very volatile
and it's challenging to do business there.

00:26:32.182 --> 00:26:36.451
&gt;&gt; I'd say the flip side to that is that
it requires you to have a lot of patience,

00:26:36.451 --> 00:26:39.600
a lot of resilience, because otherwise,
it would be easy and

00:26:39.600 --> 00:26:42.308
probably a lot of people
would be doing the same.

00:26:42.308 --> 00:26:48.038
So for us, having to deal with all that
frustration of dealing with government,

00:26:48.038 --> 00:26:54.210
with tax regimes, with unions, with all
the difficulties we have, makes it harder.

00:26:54.210 --> 00:26:58.124
&gt;&gt; It definitely makes it seem like it is
a lot harder than building a company here.

00:26:58.124 --> 00:27:02.574
What do you think the developed
world can learn from startups and

00:27:02.574 --> 00:27:06.130
entrepreneurship in emerging markets,
Stileo?

00:27:06.130 --> 00:27:08.816
&gt;&gt; I think it has to do with that,

00:27:08.816 --> 00:27:14.732
that a lot of companies that go
into Latin America, for example.

00:27:14.732 --> 00:27:21.846
They go with some ideas of how
things work in their home markets,

00:27:21.846 --> 00:27:26.474
and those aren't necessarily the same.

00:27:26.474 --> 00:27:31.696
I like to say that e-commerce,
for example, is extremely local.

00:27:31.696 --> 00:27:37.526
We need to integrate with payment
providers from each respective country,

00:27:37.526 --> 00:27:42.824
even from MercadoLibre which is
in 18 countries in Latin America.

00:27:42.824 --> 00:27:47.887
The fact that you integrate with
Visa in Brazil means nothing for

00:27:47.887 --> 00:27:49.774
Visa and in Argentina.

00:27:49.774 --> 00:27:53.681
You need to do something specific
in each of those markets.

00:27:53.681 --> 00:27:56.956
So logistics is very local.

00:27:56.956 --> 00:28:01.824
So the challenges are really
not to just bring a model that

00:28:01.824 --> 00:28:06.404
works in your home market and
expect it to work local.

00:28:06.404 --> 00:28:08.719
I think that's true of
all emerging markets.

00:28:08.719 --> 00:28:13.099
&gt;&gt; Hernan, can we get your opinion on
this localization topic now that you're

00:28:13.099 --> 00:28:16.324
on the other side as an investor
working with startups?

00:28:16.324 --> 00:28:21.442
What advantages does a local
incumbents player have in the market

00:28:21.442 --> 00:28:25.921
when in an international,
big western player comes in?

00:28:25.921 --> 00:28:30.270
I'm thinking about Amazon's aggressive
attempts to enter the region.

00:28:30.270 --> 00:28:34.456
Are there benefits unique to
the indigenous players in these parts of

00:28:34.456 --> 00:28:35.187
the world?

00:28:35.187 --> 00:28:42.140
&gt;&gt; That's a central part of our
investment, strategy in Casick.

00:28:42.140 --> 00:28:46.901
We only invest in businesses
where we think there are some,

00:28:46.901 --> 00:28:49.865
what we call local friction points.

00:28:49.865 --> 00:28:53.776
Something like what Stileo was describing,
because of payments or

00:28:53.776 --> 00:28:57.494
because of logistics or,
in FinTech, because of regulation.

00:28:57.494 --> 00:29:00.462
We are, again,
we're talking about technology.

00:29:00.462 --> 00:29:02.675
So there are no absolute advantages.

00:29:02.675 --> 00:29:05.268
So everything is very competitive.

00:29:05.268 --> 00:29:11.419
But at least, if you are the local player
and you know how to operate logistics

00:29:11.419 --> 00:29:16.438
there or you know how to get
the approval from the Central Bank,

00:29:16.438 --> 00:29:20.807
that gives you an edge versus
the outside competitor.

00:29:20.807 --> 00:29:27.428
That only has larger funds or
economic capacity but

00:29:27.428 --> 00:29:32.363
does not get that final point so well.

00:29:32.363 --> 00:29:36.586
So it's harder to develop that
if you are in the Bay Area or

00:29:36.586 --> 00:29:40.379
if you're in China or
in another part of the world.

00:29:40.379 --> 00:29:44.499
It's much easier to do it
from a local perspective.

00:29:44.499 --> 00:29:49.292
You need to be great at it and that's your
advantage but I mean your disadvantage is

00:29:49.292 --> 00:29:53.823
that your global competitor has better
access to financing or other things.

00:29:53.823 --> 00:29:57.447
So you really need to make sure
you can overcompensate for that.

00:29:57.447 --> 00:30:00.401
I think clearly,
MercadoLibre has been an example of that.

00:30:00.401 --> 00:30:06.144
And many of the companies we invest in try
to follow that same path not only to have

00:30:06.144 --> 00:30:11.635
large businesses, but those businesses
where the fact that you are local,

00:30:11.635 --> 00:30:16.901
and that you are operating from the local
market gives you an edge there.

00:30:16.901 --> 00:30:22.200
&gt;&gt; Marcos, in your experience,
what would you say is the biggest

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:27.411
mistake that an entrepreneur in
an emerging market can make?

00:30:27.411 --> 00:30:32.888
&gt;&gt; Overspending, probably, and
not focusing on building a great product.

00:30:32.888 --> 00:30:37.778
What we've seen a lot is entrepreneurs
imitating this model that you see a lot in

00:30:37.778 --> 00:30:41.364
developed markets where you
have a lot of access to funds.

00:30:41.364 --> 00:30:42.547
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; Like stories like

00:30:42.547 --> 00:30:44.560
the ones we're reading
in the newspapers now.

00:30:44.560 --> 00:30:46.430
We work for, just to say one or Uber.

00:30:46.430 --> 00:30:50.516
They've raised billions and
billions of capital.

00:30:50.516 --> 00:30:55.099
It's very rare that you can raise
that much money in emerging markets.

00:30:55.099 --> 00:30:59.237
But at some point in time, there is a lot
of liquidity in emerging markets, so

00:30:59.237 --> 00:31:02.373
people raise a lot of money in
those points in those times.

00:31:02.373 --> 00:31:06.624
And they try to recreate that strategy
of spending and eventually we will

00:31:06.624 --> 00:31:10.956
figure out the business, and
eventually we will figure out the product.

00:31:10.956 --> 00:31:14.532
And I've never seen one of those
stories succeed in the region.

00:31:14.532 --> 00:31:18.867
I've seen many, the entrepreneurs that we
see succeeding are the ones that are very

00:31:18.867 --> 00:31:20.836
focused on the product, in the team.

00:31:20.836 --> 00:31:24.834
Obviously, when the opportunity
comes to raise money or

00:31:24.834 --> 00:31:30.514
you try to raise as much as you can, but
then try to administer that capital well.

00:31:30.514 --> 00:31:35.792
Because the stories of racing a lot
of money and funding deficit for

00:31:35.792 --> 00:31:41.268
many, many years are very unlikely
to succeed in emerging markets.

00:31:41.268 --> 00:31:46.163
The funding window always ends up
closing at some point in time.

00:31:46.163 --> 00:31:49.345
&gt;&gt; But now we have so
many more of those success stories.

00:31:49.345 --> 00:31:53.189
Earlier we talked about how the landscape
wasn't there when you guys first started.

00:31:53.189 --> 00:31:55.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; But now there is this huge culture of

00:31:55.180 --> 00:31:56.671
tech entrepreneurship and

00:31:56.671 --> 00:31:59.760
it's thriving in part due to
the work that you all have done.

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:02.454
Marcos you're on the board
of Endeavor Global.

00:32:02.454 --> 00:32:05.136
&gt;&gt; Yep.
&gt;&gt; Focusing on long-term economic growth

00:32:05.136 --> 00:32:09.587
by working with entrepreneurs, and
Hernan Kaszek Ventures' mission is to

00:32:09.587 --> 00:32:12.660
work with technology
start-ups in Latin America.

00:32:12.660 --> 00:32:16.695
For those of us who are interested
in building entrepreneurial and

00:32:16.695 --> 00:32:19.865
technology ecosystems in
other parts of the world or

00:32:19.865 --> 00:32:24.490
even parts to the US that have largely
been ignored by the innovation train.

00:32:24.490 --> 00:32:28.989
What would you say are the foundational
things that you've helped

00:32:28.989 --> 00:32:34.049
implement in Latin America that we need
to focus on to foster innovation and

00:32:34.049 --> 00:32:36.479
impact in other parts of the world?

00:32:36.479 --> 00:32:40.226
&gt;&gt; I think having a success
case is very important.

00:32:40.226 --> 00:32:44.468
I mean, I see what Marco Aleeri has
done for the region has been key,

00:32:44.468 --> 00:32:47.968
because then many other
entrepreneurs can say, here,

00:32:47.968 --> 00:32:51.635
we're going to be the next
Marco Aleeri and that helps a lot.

00:32:51.635 --> 00:32:58.079
So try to build from a success story.

00:32:58.079 --> 00:33:03.090
I think that is very helpful,
particularly if you are from a region

00:33:03.090 --> 00:33:07.669
that is not associated with tech or
with entrepreneurship.

00:33:07.669 --> 00:33:12.229
Try to create I think Israel has done
that and now they have many, many,

00:33:12.229 --> 00:33:13.754
many success stories.

00:33:13.754 --> 00:33:15.782
But they started with one,
and then another one.

00:33:15.782 --> 00:33:18.522
The first it was Wayze and then etc.

00:33:18.522 --> 00:33:24.104
But so try to start with one success case
and build from there, I think that's,

00:33:24.104 --> 00:33:28.881
I don't know Amari you want-
&gt;&gt; I think what clearly the tech ecosystem

00:33:28.881 --> 00:33:34.009
in Latin America has evolved a lot,
in our early days, there was nothing.

00:33:34.009 --> 00:33:38.796
We just took advantage of that small
window when investment bankers

00:33:38.796 --> 00:33:41.996
wanted to provide some
capital to startups.

00:33:41.996 --> 00:33:46.639
But then after that, there was
a desert for many, many years and

00:33:46.639 --> 00:33:51.378
then slowly but steadily,
opportunities started to to appear.

00:33:51.378 --> 00:33:53.945
Clearly successful cases that can

00:33:53.945 --> 00:33:58.913
inspire the next generation of
entrepreneurs is very important.

00:33:58.913 --> 00:34:04.139
I think that having a few investors
that can really help those

00:34:04.139 --> 00:34:09.279
companies take off with some
advice is very, very useful.

00:34:09.279 --> 00:34:14.049
Obviously you have to have
the underlying tailwind of technology

00:34:14.049 --> 00:34:15.705
entering the market.

00:34:15.705 --> 00:34:17.974
Maybe not at the most accelerated pace,

00:34:17.974 --> 00:34:21.792
that was something I remember that
used to happen in our early days.

00:34:21.792 --> 00:34:27.695
We're always growing at a very fast rate,
but never at 500, 1,000% per year.

00:34:27.695 --> 00:34:32.109
In the initial days of our relationship
with E-Bay, that became for a time

00:34:32.109 --> 00:34:36.876
a share holder of Marco Aleeri, they were
saying they were looking at the typical

00:34:36.876 --> 00:34:41.735
charts and seeing a year one for eBay year
one for Marco Aleeri, year two for eBay.

00:34:41.735 --> 00:34:45.229
And during those years we were
growing much faster they would say.

00:34:45.229 --> 00:34:50.149
I said hey this is what we can do
today because of infrastructure,

00:34:50.149 --> 00:34:52.921
because of Internet penetration but

00:34:52.921 --> 00:34:58.022
we think that we're going to have
a much longer growth trajectory,

00:34:58.022 --> 00:35:03.658
because up until we reach the kind of
population penetration that we want,

00:35:03.658 --> 00:35:07.164
it'll take us longer, but we'll get there.

00:35:07.164 --> 00:35:09.854
And I think, that at that time,
people did not buy that.

00:35:09.854 --> 00:35:11.613
But that is what ended up happening,

00:35:11.613 --> 00:35:14.636
Marco Aleeri is still today keeps
on growing at amazing rates.

00:35:14.636 --> 00:35:18.544
And all the other comparables
grew much faster initially, but

00:35:18.544 --> 00:35:21.283
then they plateau, and that has happened.

00:35:21.283 --> 00:35:25.870
So obviously, you need to have that
underlying force that is driving that

00:35:25.870 --> 00:35:28.022
ecosystem, and then be patient.

00:35:28.022 --> 00:35:33.269
Because everything we said happened
maybe it happened a little later

00:35:33.269 --> 00:35:39.421
versus what we we thought I remember that
now the business plan we put together for

00:35:39.421 --> 00:35:43.332
our second round of financing
ended up happening.

00:35:43.332 --> 00:35:44.314
&gt;&gt; It still bid by the way.

00:35:44.314 --> 00:35:45.218
&gt;&gt; We.
[LAUGH]

00:35:45.218 --> 00:35:45.853
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:35:45.853 --> 00:35:49.070
&gt;&gt; We surpassed that big time by 35 years

00:35:49.070 --> 00:35:49.757
later.

00:35:49.757 --> 00:35:55.120
[LAUGH] So and we need to make sure
that you focus on the long run,

00:35:55.120 --> 00:36:00.436
but I think in success cases,
investor that can help you there.

00:36:00.436 --> 00:36:05.397
Marcos was mentioning regulation,
at least regulation that doesn't kill you.

00:36:05.397 --> 00:36:06.432
It's important.

00:36:06.432 --> 00:36:09.886
&gt;&gt; I would add one more thing
which is a culture of giving back.

00:36:09.886 --> 00:36:12.919
And it's not just money, but time.

00:36:12.919 --> 00:36:16.772
I think there is this
need of what did you do,

00:36:16.772 --> 00:36:21.677
what happened in your case,
can you tell us your story?

00:36:21.677 --> 00:36:25.609
I think that's something that
we've been doing a lot of,

00:36:25.609 --> 00:36:30.116
and more people, other success
stories as well in Latin America.

00:36:30.116 --> 00:36:33.416
Have been able to go back and
tell their story.

00:36:33.416 --> 00:36:38.078
Obviously in some cases, also financing
these ventures, which is great.

00:36:38.078 --> 00:36:41.688
